('2d692ea5-6084-5c21-aff5-3c28748652da', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', '5e4207c2-e358-4374-b5ab-92bbcb2b87e7', 'a-level', '2.4', '9702 coursebook 2.4 · Deducing acceleration', 45, 45, 'A v–t curve for a car obeys v = 30(1 − e^(−t/10)) m s⁻¹. (a) Calculate v at t = 10 s and t = 12 s. (b) Estimate the acceleration at about 11 s from these. (c) State what happens to the acceleration as t increases.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1', 'm s⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) v(10) = 30 × (1 − e^(−1)) = 18.96 m s⁻¹; v(12) = 20.96 m s⁻¹. (b) a = (20.96 − 18.96) ÷ 2 = 1.00 m s⁻². (c) It falls towards zero. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: v(10). C1: v(12). M1: chord gradient. C1: value. B1: decreases to zero. A1: a = 1.00 m s⁻².', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg06dd4afb-t fg06dd4afb-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg06dd4afb-t">Approaching top speed</title><desc id="fg06dd4afb-d">The curve v = 30(1 − e^(−t/10)): it rises steeply at first and levels off towards 30 m s⁻¹.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="117.6" y1="34" x2="117.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.7" y1="34" x2="205.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="293.9" y1="34" x2="293.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="29.4" y1="242" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(