('739eb063-5653-5568-87da-c17291040980', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', '5e4207c2-e358-4374-b5ab-92bbcb2b87e7', 'a-level', '2.4', '9702 coursebook 2.4 · Deducing acceleration', 48, 48, 'A cyclist''s v–t graph rises from 2.0 m s⁻¹ to 8.0 m s⁻¹ in 4.0 s, stays constant for 6.0 s, and falls to 5.0 m s⁻¹ in 3.0 s. (a) Calculate both accelerations. (b) Calculate the average acceleration over the 13 s.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.2308', 'm s⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) (8.0 − 2.0) ÷ 4.0 = 1.5 m s⁻²; (5.0 − 8.0) ÷ 3.0 = −1.0 m s⁻². (b) (5.0 − 2.0) ÷ 13 = 0.231 m s⁻². Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: first. C1: last. M1: overall Δv/Δt. C1: value. A1: a = 0.231 m s⁻².', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg26aad566-t fg26aad566-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg26aad566-t">Cyclist</title><desc id="fg26aad566-d">A v–t graph rising from 2.0 to 8.0 m s⁻¹ in 4.0 s, constant for 6.0 s, then falling to 5.0 m s⁻¹ in 3.0 s.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="79.8" y1="34" x2="79.8" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="130.2" y1="34" x2="130.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="180.5" y1="34" x2="180.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="230.9" y1="34" x2="230.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="281.3" y1="34" x2="281.3" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none