('64da4bf9-d47d-51e6-bb84-9b968353c97f', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', '5e4207c2-e358-4374-b5ab-92bbcb2b87e7', 'a-level', '2.4', '9702 coursebook 2.4 · Deducing acceleration', 52, 52, 'Readings for a car: t/s = 0, 2.0, 4.0, 6.0 and v/m s⁻¹ = 1.0, 4.0, 9.0, 16. (a) Show that the acceleration is not constant. (b) Calculate the average acceleration between 2.0 s and 6.0 s.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3', 'm s⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The changes in v over each 2.0 s are 3.0, 5.0 and 7.0 m s⁻¹, so not equal. (b) a = (16 − 4.0) ÷ (6.0 − 2.0) = 3.0 m s⁻². Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: unequal changes. B1: not constant. M1: chord gradient. C1: value. A1: a = 3.0 m s⁻².', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg5960b2df-t fg5960b2df-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg5960b2df-t">Car velocities</title><desc id="fg5960b2df-d">Table and graph of a car''s velocity: 1.0, 4.0, 9.0 and 16 m s⁻¹ at 0, 2.0, 4.0 and 6.0 s, lying on an upward curve.</desc><g transform="translate(0,0)"><rect x="130.4" y="8" width="139.1" height="140" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="130.4" y="8" width="139.1" height="28" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="158.4" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">t / s</text><text x="228" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">v / m s⁻¹</text><line x1="186.4" y1="8" x2="186.4" y2="148" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="130.4" y1="36" x2="269.6" y2="36" style="stroke