('f6521a55-44a5-542c-a952-47d9602fdc6d', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', 'c47dfb2d-b9eb-482e-b89f-aecbc8e799cc', 'a-level', '2.5', '9702 coursebook 2.5 · Deducing displacement', 46, 46, 'Car P starts from rest with acceleration 2.0 m s⁻². At the same moment car Q passes it at a steady 12 m s⁻¹. (a) Using v–t graphs, explain why P is furthest behind when their velocities are equal. (b) Calculate that greatest separation.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '36', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Until then Q is faster, so the gap grows; afterwards it shrinks. Equal at t = 12 ÷ 2.0 = 6.0 s. (b) Gap = 12 × 6.0 − 0.5 × 2.0 × 6.0² = 36 m. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: equal velocities. C1: t = 6.0 s. M1: difference of areas. C1: value. A1: Δs = 36 m.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fga3e622b2-t fga3e622b2-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fga3e622b2-t">Cars P and Q</title><desc id="fga3e622b2-d">v–t graphs: car P rising from rest with gradient 2.0 m s⁻²; car Q constant at 12 m s⁻¹. They cross at 6.0 s.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="88.2" y1="34" x2="88.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="147" y1="34" x2="147" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.7" y1="34" x2="205.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="264.5" y1="34" x2="264.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="323.2" y1="34" x2="323